LAST_SUCCESS=Task 117 M2 step 1b DONE — playback wired into the desktop host with measured drift. `--video-playback-test`: 150/150 presented, 0 dropped, drift avg 1.2ms / max 8.9ms vs a 33ms frame interval, wall 4.97s vs media 5.00s, order OK. Teeth-checked: dropping the PTS fails on 4 signals (38/150 presented, 112 dropped, wall 0.41s, order BROKEN, exit 1). Snapshot shows the sweep bar at the right edge = final frame really traveled decode->convert->surface->composite.
NOTES=Desktop decoder gained submit_for_playback/present_due/finish_playback/seek_reset + queue/drop counters. Frame-drop policy (newest-due wins) is on the HOST ADAPTER not the codec — different players want different policy. Desktop queues RGBA because frames come back to the CPU; ANDROID WILL NOT — AMediaCodec_releaseOutputBufferAtTime gives the PTS to the HW compositor and pixels never leave the GPU, so step 2 should use the native primitive rather than porting the desktop queue. STILL UNPROVEN (and unprovable on libvpx): flush()/reset() — validate on MediaCodec in step 2. NEXT: step 2 = H.264 (openh264 SW on desktop first, then HW backends), which also brings B-frames + the MediaCodec queue that finally exercise flush/reset. Remember the h264_mp4toannexb bitstream filter (~100 lines, no crate) or HW decode silently outputs nothing.
